   $q$Completing the square on $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ (with $a \ne 0$) leads to $\left(x + \frac{b}{2a}\right)^2 = $ which expression?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$\\frac{b^2 + 4ac}{4a^2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-sign-moving-constant"},{"text":"$\\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$\\frac{b^2}{4a^2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dropped-the-constant-term"},{"text":"$\\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{2a}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"did-not-square-the-denominator"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$\frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}$$q$, $q$Divide by $a$: $x^2 + \frac{b}{a}x = -\frac{c}{a}$. Add $\left(\frac{b}{2a}\right)^2 = \frac{b^2}{4a^2}$ to both sides. The right side becomes $\frac{b^2}{4a^2} - \frac{4ac}{4a^2} = \frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}$. Taking square roots then gives the quadratic formula. The expression with $+4ac$ forgets that $c$ changes sign when it moves across the equals sign.$q$, 'draft', null)
