   $q$What are the solutions to $2x^2 + 4x + 5 = 0$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$x = -1 \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dropped-the-i"},{"text":"$x = 1 \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}i$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-b-instead-of-negative-b"},{"text":"$x = -2 \\pm i\\sqrt{6}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"divided-by-a-instead-of-2a"},{"text":"$x = -1 \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}i$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$x = -1 \pm \frac{\sqrt{6}}{2}i$$q$, $q$The discriminant is $16 - 4(2)(5) = -24$, and $\sqrt{-24} = 2i\sqrt{6}$. So $x = \frac{-4 \pm 2i\sqrt{6}}{4} = -1 \pm \frac{\sqrt{6}}{2}i$. The answer $-2 \pm i\sqrt{6}$ divides by $2$ instead of $2a = 4$.$q$, 'draft', null)
